The comment `@@@ If the index is valid, the returned value is the element that was at that position before the update.` specifies that when the index is valid, the method returns the old value at that index.

We can express this using `\old(this.get(index))` to refer to the value before the method call, and compare it to `\result`, which represents the return value.

Since the method checks `Objects.checkIndex(index, size)` which ensures the index is valid, the premise is that the index is within bounds (i.e., `0 <= index < this.size()`), and under that condition, the result must equal the old value at that index.

Thus, the correct assert statement is:

<code>assert (0 <= index && index < this.size()) => \result.equals(\old(this.get(index)));</code>
